WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:07.839
It often takes a whole bunch of creative minds
to make a movie happen, and they don't always

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:10.000
have a unified vision.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.420
The director and screenwriter might disagree
over how the story should go, or studio execs

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:19.390
might make changes after test audiences decide
that seeing the Titanic sink is just too sad.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:23.550
Were these movies improved by the changes
they went through on their way to the big

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:24.550
screen?

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:28.039
Fortunately, their original endings have survived,
so we can judge for ourselves.

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:29.970
Terminator 2: Judgment Day

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:34.430
It's now a massive franchise spanning half
a dozen films and a TV series.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.690
But director James Cameron wrapped up the
story he wanted to tell with Terminator 2.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.010
Or at least he tried to.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.690
The film initially ended with an epilogue
set 30 years in the future.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:49.580
Sarah Connor tells the audience that the "Judgment
Day" of August 29, 1997 came and went without

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.440
any sign of a robot-human war.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.409
John Connor is a senator and a dad.

00:00:53.409 --> 00:00:58.170
"The luxury of hope was given to me by the
Terminator."

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.470
It offered a satisfying conclusion after the
frightening emotional rollercoaster of the

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:02.710
first two films.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:06.650
But studio bosses didn't like the epilogue
because it meant no more time travel, no more

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.850
robots…and no more sequels.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.610
"I'll be back."

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:12.610
Titanic

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.040
"I'll never let go.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:17.300
I promise."

00:01:21.719 --> 00:01:26.640
Another James Cameron movie, Titanic concludes
on a sweet, romantic note: Old Rose throws

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.880
the Heart of the Ocean overboard while on
a boat full of explorers actively looking

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.370
for that very gem.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:37.060
Then she goes to sleep and dies and is reunited
with Jack, the great love of her life.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.390
It's probably a better ending than the first
one Cameron wrote.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:43.360
Old Rose is caught by the crew throwing the
Heart of the Ocean into the sea.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.030
The explorers go nuts.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:49.790
And then Bill Paxton laughs like a fool.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.110
The Butterfly Effect

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.430
The good news: Ashton Kutcher can time travel.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:58.420
The bad news: His constant manipulation of
time, as well as his general existence, messes

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.370
up the lives of everyone around him.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:04.460
So, at the end of The Butterfly Effect, to
spare his one true love the pain he caused

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.120
her, he goes back in time and makes sure they
never meet.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.840
But the film initially called for the character
to go much, much, much farther back in time

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.420
to prevent the damage he caused.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.420
Like, all the way back.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.959
He goes back to his own gestating body and
strangles himself with the umbilical cord.

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:20.959
Gross.

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:22.779
The Blair Witch Project

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.830
This found footage horror flick ends with
Heather finding her film school buddy stuck

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:40.380
in the corner of a derelict basement facing
the wall, just before she gets knocked out

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.760
by some unseen menace.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.400
This lives up to the Blair Witch murder motif
they learned from the locals earlier.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.630
The original ending is more to the point,
with Heather finding Mike's hanging body instead.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:50.630
Clerks

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:54.490
Kevin Smith's directorial debut depicted a
day in the life—with a few side adventures—of

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:56.050
a convenience store clerk.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:59.900
His original ending finished the film on a
dark note that's also a sadly unavoidable

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:03.819
occupational hazard for anyone standing behind
a retail counter:

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:04.579
"Oh, I'm sorry.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:05.080
We're closed."

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.849
Dante is shot and killed in a robbery.

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:16.209
Just think: if he'd kept that ending, we never
would've gotten to see the cinematic masterpiece

00:03:16.209 --> 00:03:17.590
that was Clerks II.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:19.020
Thanks for watching!

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.319
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:24.959
And leave us a comment to let us know which
movie you think would’ve been better with

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:25.720
its other ending.

